"\nTARGET 3 .claude/rules/instruction\u002dfiles.md prose\u002dentrypoint\nIDENTITY 7cc484b09a3f8ceab47e59f37fbbba8f354403b1 ca638c32b6bbf991c02a865b9c3ab19279416806e7baa0a320713989610da845 RANGES [[1, 225]] MASKED [164]\n1: \u002d\u002d\u002d\n2: description: \"Where the canonical rules live and how they load; how to tell a stale worktree copy from the current one.\"\n3: paths:\n4:   \u002d \"**\"\n5: \u002d\u002d\u002d\n6: \n7: # Where the rules live, and which copy you are reading\n8: \n9: Every rule in this directory loads automatically: those with a `paths:` glob\n10: when a matching file enters context, those without one at session start. There\n11: is no second copy \u2014 `.github/instructions/` was a Copilot convention and is\n12: gone, along with Copilot.\n13: \n14: Other tools in use here read the same files: Crush can be pointed at this\n15: folder with `global\u002dcontext\u002dpath`, and it already discovers `.claude/skills`.\n16: Crush loads the folder recursively and does **not** honour `paths:`, so every\n17: rule reaches it every session \u2014 a rule that is noise there is noise always.\n18: \n19: ## The front\u002dmatter standard\n20: \n21: Every file in this directory opens with front matter, and nothing else comes\n22: before it:\n23: \n24: ```yaml\n25: \u002d\u002d\u002d\n26: description: \"<one line: what this file decides, and when to read it>\"\n27: paths:\n28:   \u002d \"<glob>\"          # optional; omit the whole key to load every session\n29: \u002d\u002d\u002d\n30: ```\n31: \n32: \u002d **`description` is one line**, and it is the only thing a reader sees in an\n33:   index or a picker. Say what the file decides and when to open it, not what\n34:   subject area it belongs to. (`council\u002dsubstrate\u002dintegrity.md` carries a\n35:   longer one; it is adopted verbatim from another estate and is refreshed by\n36:   deliberate re\u002dreview, not edited here \u2014 #147 \u00a7 9.)\n37: \u002d **`description` comes first**, then `paths:`. The order changes nothing\n38:   mechanically; it makes the files diffable against each other.\n39: \u002d **`paths:` is optional, and omitting it is a decision.** A file with no\n40:   `paths:` key loads at session start, every session. A file that omits it\n41:   **says so in its first paragraph, and why** \u2014 otherwise a reader scanning\n42:   front matter for \"when does this apply\" finds nothing and assumes the\n43:   answer is \"never\".\n44: \u002d **A comment above a glob explains a glob that is wider or narrower than it\n45:   looks.** `#` comments are legal inside the block and are where the #147\n46:   sweep's reasoning lives (`rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md`, the four ES files).\n47: \u002d **A narrow glob is the only kind that can miss.** `paths: \u002d \"**\"` matches\n48:   every file in this repo, so in practice it means \"always\" for any session\n49:   that touches the tree. Crush ignores `paths:` entirely and loads the whole\n50:   directory, so a narrow glob only ever costs Claude Code.\n51: \n52: ## The ES rules reach an ES session only by loading every session\n53: \n54: The four EmulationStation rules \u2014 `es\u002dnative\u002dui.md`, `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext.md`,\n55: `es\u002dcode\u002dtraps.md`, `es\u002dui\u002dstyle\u002dguide.md` \u2014 govern work in a **different\n56: repository**: ES source is `ROCKNIX/emulationstation\u002dnext`, checked out\n57: elsewhere on the machine. A `paths:` glob is resolved against this repo, so no\n58: glob written here can name `es\u002dapp/**`, and there is no narrow, high\u002dsignal\n59: glob to be had. They therefore carry `paths: \u002d \"**\"`, the widest reach\n60: available, which loads them whenever any file in this repo enters context.\n61: \n62: D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d010 keeps one canonical distribution rule corpus. Each active ES\n63: checkout carries `AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` entry pointers to the distribution\n64: checkout's `next` rules and `.github/sessions/saved\u002dsession\u002dstate\u002dnext.md`.\n65: They name the ES syntax check and integration branch; they do not duplicate\n66: policy. The splash repository carries the same resume route in `AGENTS.md`.\n67: Read those pointers at entry, then the canonical files they name (#368).\n68: \n69: **Seeing `.github/instructions/` means you are in a stale worktree.** That\n70: directory no longer exists on `next`. Several worktrees were cut before it was\n71: retired and still carry it on disk, so its presence is a reliable signal that\n72: the rules around you predate the move \u2014 not that a second copy exists.\n73: \n74: **Read rules and skills from `next`, not from a feature worktree.** A branch\n75: cut from an older base silently lacks anything added since. This has bitten\n76: twice: ES work proceeded in a worktree missing `es\u002dnative\u002dui`, and a code audit\n77: loaded a stale copy of its own skill and would have graded the work against a\n78: rubric that no longer existed.\n79: \n80: ```bash\n81: diff \u002dq <file> <(git \u002dC /workspace/repos/rocknix show next:<file>)\n82: ```\n83: \n84: ## The index\n85: \n86: Every rule file, what it decides, and what makes it load. Add a row here in the\n87: same change that adds a file; a rule nobody can find is a rule nobody applies.\n88: \n89: | File | Decides | Loads |\n90: | \u002d\u002d\u002d | \u002d\u002d\u002d | \u002d\u002d\u002d |\n91: | `least\u002dsurprise.md` | the player is surprised as little as possible; things work as expected and the same way every time (D\u002dUI\u002d042) | every session |\n92: | `player\u002dlanguage.md` | clear, then brief, then sized to the space, for every string a player reads (D\u002dUI\u002d045) | every session |\n93: | `time\u002dto\u002dplay.md` | interface \u2192 a game's first frame, and one game's exit \u2192 the next, as a first\u002dclass metric (D\u002dCLOUD\u002d098) | every session |\n94: | `vm\u002dfirst.md` | *can this be done on the VM?* asked and answered in writing before any test, proof or measurement (D\u002dQA\u002d007) | every session |\n95: | `bugs\u002dare\u002dagent\u002dfirst.md` | what a bug is: fixed to the best of our ability means fixed; every criterion verified on the VM by an agent; the two other classes (open item to test, keep an eye on); no known bug at the call (D\u002dQA\u002d051) | every session |\n96: | `ceremonies.md` | which ceremony is owed and when \u002d\u002d friction entries, retros, the weekly and monthly summaries, the index, audits, futros \u002d\u002d as a state machine `tools/ceremony\u002dcheck` turns (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d028) | every session |\n97: | `release\u002dcandidates.md` | the standard operating procedure for every release candidate: nothing behind before the cut, a clean baseline, the candidate's build, every test, play\u002dtesting, the call, the two\u002dagent upstream audit, then the submission and builds for every test device (D\u002dWORKFLOW\u002d047) | every session |\n98: | `adversarial\u002dcouncil.md` | code audits use local, cross\u002dlab two\u002dmodel or extended three\u002dmodel review; formal councils keep five verified seats | `**` |\n99: | `decision\u002dregister.md` | when a decision becomes a row, and why a settled one is cited rather than re\u002dargued | `**` |\n100: | `device\u002dbuilds.md` | building, publishing and flashing handheld images, and what a warm build root hides | `**` |\n101: | `documentation\u002daccuracy.md` | user\u002dfacing behaviour and the public rocknix.org docs change together | `**` |\n102: | `engineering\u002dpractices.md` | verify the artifact not the report; guards fail closed; nothing runs on a person's device without their yes | `**` |\n103: | `es\u002dcode\u002dtraps.md` | the sharp edges of the ES codebase, each found by debugging and each with its fix | `**` (see \u00a7 The ES rules) |\n104: | `es\u002dnative\u002dui.md` | ES mechanics: where the code lives, the page and job patterns, spacing, the four surface tiers | `**` (see \u00a7 The ES rules) |\n105: | `es\u002dplayer\u002dtext.md` | every word a player reads: the tiers, the verbs, the naming conventions, the outcome vocabulary | `**` (see \u00a7 The ES rules) |\n106: | `es\u002dui\u002dstyle\u002dguide.md` | how a screen looks and behaves: row builders, gates, buttons, confirmations, glyphs, wizards | `**` (see \u00a7 The ES rules) |\n107: | `fork\u002dworkflow.md` | the branch model, and building an upstream PR by content so no personal path leaks | `**` |\n108: | `instruction\u002dfiles.md` | where the rules live, how they load, the front\u002dmatter standard, and this index | `**` |\n109: | `issue\u002dtracking.md` | issues on the fork only; Milestone \u2192 Epic \u2192 Issue; what a ticked acceptance criterion means | `**` |\n110: | `milestone\u002dphase\u002dnaming.md` | milestone bodies hold current ordered priorities; M/P issue titles identify placement, not issue chronology | `**` |\n111: | `learning\u002dcapture.md` | a learning becomes a rule, a tool or a work\u002dlog entry \u2014 never only a memory | `**` |\n112: | `upgrade\u002dand\u002dinstall.md` | every change lands on a device that already has state; check the upgrade and the clean install | `**` |\n113: | `worktrees.md` | one worktree per branch under `../rocknix.worktrees/`, build worktrees on `build/*`, removal via `tools/fork\u002dworktree` | `**` |\n114: | `working\u002dprinciples.md` | the twelve principles and which rule enforces each \u002d\u002d an index, not a second copy; the pre\u002dflight (read the rules the work touches, this session) and the enforcement ladder | `**` |\n115: | `packaging\u002dand\u002dpatches.md` | `package.mk` fields, late binding, and how patches are produced and scoped | `packages/**`, `projects/**` |\n116: | `change\u002dlog.md` | the running change log (`docs/cloud\u002dsync\u002dchangelog.md`) is written the day a player\u002dvisible change lands, as claims checked against the build | `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/**`, the change log |\n117: | `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md` | the cloud\u002dsync subsystem: config conventions, the bounded automatic sync, last\u002dgood behaviour | the rclone package, `rocknix/sources/scripts/**`, the five cloud tools |\n118: | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` | building and QA'ing the GENERIC_X64 VM image, and the harness that drives it | GENERIC_X64, `projects/ROCKNIX/packages/**`, `scripts/mkimage`, `scripts/image`, the VM tools, `docs/vm\u002dqa\u002dlog.md` |\n119: | `handheld\u002devidence.md` | what a handheld keeps across a power cycle and what to capture first when one misbehaves | device packages and kernels, `docs/**` |\n120: | `council\u002dsubstrate\u002dintegrity.md` | every council member call goes through the Facilitator; no ad\u002dhoc provider calls | council artifacts, skills, agents, `scripts/council\u002dinvoke.ts` and `scripts/lib/council\u002dverification.ts` |\n121: \n122: Three documents under `docs/` carry interface law and load **nowhere** \u2014 open\n123: them when the work is theirs: `docs/es\u002dmenu\u002dmap.md` (where a row belongs; and\n124: D\u002dUI\u002d039, a row added, moved or renamed updates it in the same change),\n125: `docs/conflict\u002dwizard\u002dia.md` (the wizard's IA), `docs/device\u002dtesting\u002dpolicy.md`\n126: (the device and QA\u002dguest policy, whose read filter is now also in\n127: `engineering\u002dpractices.md`).\n128: \n129: **The principles behind these rules, and which rule enforces each, are in\n130: [`working\u002dprinciples.md`](working\u002dprinciples.md)** \u2014 an index, not a second\n131: copy. Read it when you want to know whether something is already covered\n132: before writing a new rule; ten of twelve imported principles turned out to be.\n133: \n134: ## The fork's own tools, and which rule documents each\n135: \n136: Written because a tool nobody remembers is a tool nobody runs \u2014 and this\n137: estate keeps growing. One line each; the rule named is where the detail lives, so this stays an index rather than a second copy. `tools/` is\n138: otherwise upstream's, which is why the fork\u002donly ones are enumerated by hand\n139: in `.githooks/pre\u002dpush` and in `fork\u002dworkflow.md`; **a new one is added to\n140: both lists and to this table, or it is invisible.**\n141: \n142: | Tool | What it answers | Detail in |\n143: | \u002d\u002d\u002d | \u002d\u002d\u002d | \u002d\u002d\u002d |\n144: | `build\u002dpreflight` | has the machine the memory for a build; explicit guarded swap reclamation before launch | `device\u002dbuilds.md` |\n145: | `host\u002dmaintenance/` | fixed\u002dtarget swap helper, narrow administrator installer and isolated guard tests | `device\u002dbuilds.md` |\n146: | `archaeology` | what the record already says about a question \u002d\u002d the registers, the work logs, the rules, `git log`, the issues \u002d\u002d before anything is called pending or new | `decision\u002dregister.md` |\n147: | `ceremony\u002dcheck` | which ceremony is owed (a friction entry's issue, a retro, a weekly or monthly summary, the index, a blindspot's guard, an audit, a futro) and whether the push guard refuses | `ceremonies.md` |\n148: | `frame\u002ddiff` | did this build change any walk frame it did not mean to \u002d\u002d the boxes against the last accepted cut, the masks, the claims | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n149: | `work\u002dlog\u002dindex` | the day, week and month table of contents over the work logs, regenerated after every entry | `learning\u002dcapture.md` |\n150: | `watch\u002djob` | is a long job still alive, stalled, finished, or killed \u2014 and is the watcher itself alive | `engineering\u002dpractices.md` |\n151: | `watch\u002dbuild` | automatically arm the shared watcher for native/Docker builds and retain each run's logs, PID and result | `device\u002dbuilds.md` |\n152: | `watch\u002dbuild\u002dsubmit` | submit the standard runner independently of its shell; distinguish launch from eventual completion | `engineering\u002dpractices.md` |\n153: | `fork\u002dworktree` | worktree list / remove / repair / sync, refusing to destroy build output | `worktrees.md` |\n154: | `fork\u002dpackage\u002dfreshness` | are the packages the fork introduces at their latest upstream release, or pinned with a stated reason | `fork\u002dworkflow.md` |\n155: | `vm\u002dupgrade\u002drehearsal`, `vm\u002dupgrade\u002dreadiness\u002dtest` | boot the previous image in a guest, seed a player's state, update in place, check every piece survived | `upgrade\u002dand\u002dinstall.md` |\n156: | `cloud\u002dpair\u002dmigration` | the mixed\u002dinstallation test: one cloud, a device updated in place from the previous build and a fresh install, the folder's join, move and follow read from the cloud and both confs (D\u002dCLOUD\u002d158) | `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md` |\n157: | `es\u002dsyntax\u002dcheck` | compile an EmulationStation edit syntax\u002donly with the image build's own command, before the pin moves | `es\u002dnative\u002dui.md` |\n158: | `png\u002dblackout` | paint a rectangle of a frame black with the standard library, so a screendump carrying the QA account's name is filed with the band painted out | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n159: | `fork\u002dnewdrive` | move the build estate to another volume | `device\u002dbuilds.md` |\n160: | `fork\u002dpublish\u002drelease` | prepare a release plan from exact candidate bytes, with selected\u002dstate inspection; never publishes | `device\u002dbuilds.md` |\n161: | `pixelelated\u002dstate\u002dcheck` | inspect selected account, cloud and history state in final payloads or a ready guest; value\u002dfree reports | `release\u002dcandidates.md` |\n162: | `pixelelated\u002dstate\u002dcheck\u002dtest` | synthetic clean/contaminated roots and payloads, plus prepared first\u002dboot proof | `release\u002dcandidates.md` |\n163: | `device\u002dact` | run one command on a device, recorded with the boot id before and after | `engineering\u002dpractices.md` |\n164: [MASKED sensitive\u002dlabelled line; not read unredacted]\n165: | `vm\u002dpair`, `vm\u002dpair\u002dtest`, `vm\u002dserial`, `vm\u002dvisual\u002dqa`, `vm\u002dwalks/` | bring guests up, test launch failures, drive them, capture frames, walk the interface | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n166: | `vm\u002dqa` | every automated check against one image, one report | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n167: | `vm\u002dstop`, `vm\u002dstop\u002dtest` | stop only the owned QEMU and await exit; exercise delayed exit, timeout and refusal controls | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n168: | `vm\u002dmanager\u002dsystem\u002dcheck` | refuse manager walkthroughs on an absent or wrongly selected system | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n169: | `cloud\u002dround\u002dtrip`, `cloud\u002dtest\u002dbackend`, `cloud\u002dcensus`, `cloud\u002dcapture\u002dstamp\u002dtest` | the cloud\u002dsync suites and their backends | `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md` |\n170: | `cloud\u002dconnections\u002dtest`, `test\u002dfixtures/` | staged connection transactions, real\u002drclone OAuth state and shutdown cleanup, with synthetic dependencies (#537/#540) | `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md` |\n171: | `cloud\u002dinput\u002dtest` | open/protected Wi\u002dFi selection and ordered phone input under delayed HTTP (#535/#536) | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n172: | `cloud\u002dsave\u002dintegrity`, `cloud\u002dsave\u002dintegrity\u002dtest` | bounded offline save\u002dbyte/container verification and synthetic corruption controls | `rclone\u002dcloud\u002dsync.md`, `docs/cloud\u002dsave\u002dintegrity.md` |\n173: | `settings\u002dmodes\u002dtest` | do shell settings and recovery writers preserve private modes and refuse failed staging with the image applets (#421) | `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md` |\n174: | `melonds\u002dupgrade\u002dtest` | does post\u002dupdate preserve melonDS settings while